WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.583
(upbeat music)

00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:06.360
- What's up, everybody? Michael here.

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:07.760
It's been nearly a decade

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:10.990
since the most infamous
restaurant review of all time.

00:00:10.990 --> 00:00:12.310
It included classic zingers,

00:00:12.310 --> 00:00:14.457
like how the blue watermelon margarita

00:00:14.457 --> 00:00:15.860
"tastes like some combination

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:18.250
of radiator fluid and formaldehyde."

00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:20.270
And the one that asks: "Why
is one of the few things

00:00:20.270 --> 00:00:22.920
on your menu that can be
eaten without fear or regret

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:25.990
called a roasted pork banh mi
when it resembles that item

00:00:25.990 --> 00:00:28.550
about as much as you
resemble Emily Dickinson?"

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:30.670
The review in question eviscerated

00:00:30.670 --> 00:00:34.200
Guy's American Kitchen and
Bar, owned by Guy Fieri.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:37.050
The review-slash-assassination
attempt was penned

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:39.680
by "New York Times"
restaurant critic Pete Wells.

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:41.060
When it came out in 2012,

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:43.970
Fieri had already been a Food
Network star for six years

00:00:43.970 --> 00:00:45.940
with several seasons of "Guy's Big Bite"

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:48.630
and "Diners, Drive-Ins,
and Dives" under his belt.

00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:50.980
He also owned four successful restaurants.

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:53.877
Today, "Diners, Drive-Ins, and
Dives" is in its 35th season.

00:00:53.877 --> 00:00:55.587
- That's a great wing. Mmm!

00:00:55.587 --> 00:00:56.630
- [Michael] "Guy's Grocery Games"

00:00:56.630 --> 00:00:58.680
has enjoyed almost 30 seasons,

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:00.680
and Fieri's face has graced scores

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:02.320
of other Food Network shows.

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:04.900
And there are plenty of
Fieri haters out there,

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:08.177
writing him off as all
bombast and, in Wells's words,

00:01:08.177 --> 00:01:11.230
"a representation of going insane."

00:01:11.230 --> 00:01:13.587
And don't forget the time
Anthony Bourdain wondered,

00:01:13.587 --> 00:01:16.040
"How does Guy Fieri de-douche?"

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:18.860
But despite the haters,
Fieri's star continues

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:20.930
to shine brighter than
a kitchen grease fire.

00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:22.810
Is this guy just the
result of what happens

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:25.850
when a can of Red Bull and a
jar of hot sauce make a baby?

00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:29.340
Can this guy, yes, this
Guy, actually teach us a lot

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:31.520
about the cultural function of food?

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:33.547
Let's find out in this
"Wisecrack Edition":

00:01:33.547 --> 00:01:35.900
"Guy Fieri: Deep or Dumb?"

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:39.170
It's no secret that Guy
Fieri is a busy dude,

00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:40.927
and as the title of his flagship show

00:01:40.927 --> 00:01:43.540
"Diners, Drive-Ins, and Dives" suggests,

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:45.010
Fieri is a champion of food

00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:47.090
that's generally considered lowbrow.

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:49.140
- It is seriously too
legit to quit, though.

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:50.990
- And because he is genuinely psyched

00:01:50.990 --> 00:01:52.960
to eat a jalapeno pepper popper sandwich

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:55.200
made by an unknown chef,
it's easy for critics

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:57.610
to write him off as a bombastic goofball.

00:01:57.610 --> 00:01:59.020
That puts him in stark contrast

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:00.820
with his fellow Food Network stars,

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:04.830
who posture as serious chefs
or bastions of fine dining,

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:08.160
like the casual culinary queen
of East Hampton, Ina Garten,

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:10.600
or the ginger master of
the restaurant kitchen,

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:12.540
Mr. Robert William Flay.

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:15.830
But Fieri is a longtime
restaurateur in his own right,

00:02:15.830 --> 00:02:18.160
and his recipes have a consistent ethos,

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:20.540
firmly rooted in a lack of concern

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:22.360
over traditional food preparations

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:25.400
and a passion for
inventive culinary fusion.

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:28.000
Aside from showing you the
Fieri tattoo over my heart,

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:31.010
it is difficult to overstate
what a singular presence he is

00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:32.520
in the celebrity chef landscape.

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:35.270
But to truly understand
the taste of Flavortown,

00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:37.200
it's important to assess
him in the context

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:39.680
of our national history
of celebrity chefs.

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:41.070
America wasn't always obsessed

00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:43.680
with watching people cook
outrageous things on TV.

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:45.140
In fact, the act of eating

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:47.660
in mainstream, post-World War II America

00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:50.730
was all about convenience and economy,

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:53.510
unless you were lucky enough
to have a bomb-ass Italian nona

00:02:53.510 --> 00:02:55.050
to serve you up some rigatoni.

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:58.230
As French chef Jacques Pepin
described in his memoir,

00:02:58.230 --> 00:03:01.100
in America, "food was
sustenance, full stop."

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:04.070
That meant lots of cheap,
highly processed eats.

00:03:04.070 --> 00:03:06.910
But the arrival of French
cooking in mainstream America

00:03:06.910 --> 00:03:09.400
would conjure a new cultural preoccupation

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:12.650
with things like food quality,
freshness, and seasonality.

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:15.830
The first big American
celebrity chef was Julia Child,

00:03:15.830 --> 00:03:18.640
who brought her training
at Le Cordon Bleu back home

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:21.270
to teach her audience that
French dishes tasted better

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:23.850
than cream-of-mushroom-soup-based
casseroles.

00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:25.680
Her act was revelatory,

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:27.450
showing that fancy cooking techniques

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:30.110
like mincing a mirepoix or deboning a duck

00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:31.720
weren't just for the professionals.

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:35.380
Also, she invented shark
repellent for the Navy.

00:03:35.380 --> 00:03:37.320
Seriously, Google it.

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:40.560
After her show, "The French
Chef," debuted in 1963,

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:43.210
public appetite for food TV mushroomed,

00:03:43.210 --> 00:03:45.140
and from the mid-'80s to the mid-'90s,

00:03:45.140 --> 00:03:47.850
foodie culture became a
mainstay of American life,

00:03:47.850 --> 00:03:51.000
coinciding with the Reagan
era and the yuppie boom.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:53.540
This eventually led to the
creation of the Food Network.

00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:57.310
Founded in 1993, it boasted
hosts like Emeril Lagasse,

00:03:57.310 --> 00:03:59.930
Jacques Pepin, and Robin Leach.

00:03:59.930 --> 00:04:01.820
And by the time the
channel was popular enough

00:04:01.820 --> 00:04:03.860
to host "The Next Food Network Star,"

00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:05.650
its stable of celebrity chefs

00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:08.170
represented something very specific.

00:04:08.170 --> 00:04:10.633
Sure, they each had their own personality-

00:04:10.633 --> 00:04:11.466
- Bam! Bam!

00:04:11.466 --> 00:04:13.230
- [Michael] and areas of expertise.

00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:16.030
But they were unified in a
vibe that dominates the network

00:04:16.030 --> 00:04:18.670
and celebrity chef culture to this day.

00:04:18.670 --> 00:04:20.310
They were seasoned, experienced,

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:23.130
and focused on elevating
the dishes they were making.

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:24.820
Sometimes, that means staying loyal

00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:27.260
to the traditional
preparation methods of a dish.

00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:30.840
Sometimes, it means adding
an unexpected fancy twist.

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:32.290
- I like to take something
really traditional

00:04:32.290 --> 00:04:33.617
and do it with a twist, and I thought,

00:04:33.617 --> 00:04:35.880
"Wouldn't it be fun to take the croutons

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:37.090
and put it on the outside?"

00:04:37.090 --> 00:04:39.610
- [Michael] Or, as "Spectacular
Foodscapes" authors

00:04:39.610 --> 00:04:41.447
Josee Johnson and Michael
Goodman would put it,

00:04:41.447 --> 00:04:44.130
"Food media frames and
mediates what foodscapes could

00:04:44.130 --> 00:04:46.910
and should be, how they
could and should operate,

00:04:46.910 --> 00:04:49.130
and for whom they could and should work.

00:04:49.130 --> 00:04:51.900
They communicate specific
food instructions via recipes,

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:54.390
but they also project powerful images

00:04:54.390 --> 00:04:56.340
of what constitutes a good meal,

00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:59.270
a worthy home cook,
and a celebrated chef."

00:04:59.270 --> 00:05:00.103
That is to say,

00:05:00.103 --> 00:05:02.950
food influencers set the
bar for home cooking,

00:05:02.950 --> 00:05:06.060
and the rest of us just
try to capture their vibes.

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:09.080
In this context of good
meals and celebrated chefs,

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:10.540
it's no wonder that the discourse

00:05:10.540 --> 00:05:13.380
around Guy Fieri has remained spicy.

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:16.560
We live in a culture that
assigns moral value to food.

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:18.600
As a result, good food can mean anything

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:21.790
from healthy to fancy to healthy-fancy,

00:05:21.790 --> 00:05:24.370
and this is where Fieri
breaks the Jell-O mold.

00:05:24.370 --> 00:05:27.070
The majority of the food
he showcases is neither low

00:05:27.070 --> 00:05:30.150
in cholesterol or high
in its opinion of itself.

00:05:30.150 --> 00:05:32.890
Truffle oil is supplanted by donkey sauce.

00:05:32.890 --> 00:05:36.000
And we think that's a
crucial part of his appeal.

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:39.170
His only criteria for a good
meal are if it tastes...

00:05:39.170 --> 00:05:41.490
- Aw, that's ridiculous!

00:05:41.490 --> 00:05:44.390
- And, of course, as far as
that celebrated-chef thing goes,

00:05:44.390 --> 00:05:46.910
we have that infamous
review, among many others,

00:05:46.910 --> 00:05:49.550
to cast aspersion upon his credentials.

00:05:49.550 --> 00:05:52.130
But the discourse around
Fieri doesn't stop there.

00:05:52.130 --> 00:05:53.920
Scholar Emily Contois noted

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:56.480
in her book "Diners, Dudes, and Diets"

00:05:56.480 --> 00:05:58.090
that as of spring 2020,

00:05:58.090 --> 00:06:00.560
the food site "Eater" had
published more features

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:03.680
about Fieri than any other big-name chef,

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:07.480
be it Anthony Bourdain,
David Chang, or anybody else,

00:06:07.480 --> 00:06:09.700
and these posts generated more than twice

00:06:09.700 --> 00:06:12.880
as many comments than any other chefs.

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:14.630
So why the disparity?

00:06:14.630 --> 00:06:16.910
We think it comes down
to the cultural context

00:06:16.910 --> 00:06:18.770
Guy Fieri is operating within.

00:06:18.770 --> 00:06:22.060
It's all fun and "Guy's Grocery
Games" for the hoi polloi

00:06:22.060 --> 00:06:24.770
who think Fieri is a
cool, down-to-earth dude

00:06:24.770 --> 00:06:26.320
they'd like to get a beer with.

00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:27.870
But that's precisely why it's so easy

00:06:27.870 --> 00:06:29.530
for critics to dunk on him.

00:06:29.530 --> 00:06:30.900
He's easily defined as part

00:06:30.900 --> 00:06:32.330
of what scholar Dwight Macdonald

00:06:32.330 --> 00:06:34.270
would call the mass culture,

00:06:34.270 --> 00:06:37.230
or its more sinister moniker, masscult.

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:39.320
That's essentially a commercialized

00:06:39.320 --> 00:06:40.970
and totally artificial culture

00:06:40.970 --> 00:06:42.940
popularized by the mass media.

00:06:42.940 --> 00:06:44.610
Masscult exists in opposition

00:06:44.610 --> 00:06:46.760
to what Macdonald calls high culture,

00:06:46.760 --> 00:06:49.210
which he characterizes as
more of the formal things

00:06:49.210 --> 00:06:52.260
you learn about in school,
like Shakespeare or the viola.

00:06:52.260 --> 00:06:53.320
In the case of food,

00:06:53.320 --> 00:06:55.890
that means high culture
is culinary school,

00:06:55.890 --> 00:06:58.030
whereas masscult is home cooking

00:06:58.030 --> 00:07:00.260
or picking up skills in a diner kitchen.

00:07:00.260 --> 00:07:02.430
And while both the
masscult and high culture

00:07:02.430 --> 00:07:04.890
can be wildly popular
among the greater public,

00:07:04.890 --> 00:07:07.317
Macdonald argued way back in 1960,

00:07:07.317 --> 00:07:10.000
"in the United States, the
demands of the audience,

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:12.740
which has changed from a
small body of connoisseurs

00:07:12.740 --> 00:07:15.070
into a large body of ignoramuses,

00:07:15.070 --> 00:07:18.270
have become the chief
criteria of success."

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:20.450
This was an exceedingly rude way

00:07:20.450 --> 00:07:22.710
of saying that taste had been democratized

00:07:22.710 --> 00:07:26.090
and thus was driven by the
consumer demands of the masses.

00:07:26.090 --> 00:07:28.700
That's how McDonald's, the
restaurant, not the scholar,

00:07:28.700 --> 00:07:31.260
has managed to serve hundreds
of billions of Big Macs

00:07:31.260 --> 00:07:34.370
without serving a McRatatouille even once.

00:07:34.370 --> 00:07:35.910
And when applying this framework

00:07:35.910 --> 00:07:37.720
to television food celebrity culture,

00:07:37.720 --> 00:07:40.440
it explains why the Food
Network airs so many hours

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:44.800
of Fieri content: he is exactly
what the spectator wants.

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:46.877
In other words, Fieri has some big

00:07:46.877 --> 00:07:49.340
"let people like what they like" energy.

00:07:49.340 --> 00:07:52.240
However, the irony is that
while Guy Fieri himself

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:54.010
might be a product of masscult,

00:07:54.010 --> 00:07:55.880
he spends many of his on-screen hours

00:07:55.880 --> 00:07:57.430
highlighting restaurants owned

00:07:57.430 --> 00:07:59.580
not by millionaire celebrity chefs

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:01.700
or backed by groups of financiers,

00:08:01.700 --> 00:08:04.830
but independent local
spots owned by families

00:08:04.830 --> 00:08:06.870
and food-obsessed non-celebrities.

00:08:06.870 --> 00:08:08.660
The implicit message seems to be

00:08:08.660 --> 00:08:09.870
even in parts of the country

00:08:09.870 --> 00:08:12.740
dominated by chain restaurants
and big-box stores,

00:08:12.740 --> 00:08:15.870
there are still amazing,
unique meals to be had

00:08:15.870 --> 00:08:17.600
if you're willing to find them.

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:19.790
Yet, despite being an easy target,

00:08:19.790 --> 00:08:21.750
Guy Fieri staunchly refuses

00:08:21.750 --> 00:08:23.960
to be anything other than what he is:

00:08:23.960 --> 00:08:26.720
someone who loves food, his family,

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:28.610
and the unassuming mom-and-pop joints

00:08:28.610 --> 00:08:31.390
that, to him, are
quintessentially American.

00:08:31.390 --> 00:08:33.000
And when he tries their signature dishes,

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:35.100
whether it's a traditional
take on a club sandwich

00:08:35.100 --> 00:08:37.710
or a modern update on
ramen, he's not trying

00:08:37.710 --> 00:08:40.040
to change them or upgrade their fanciness

00:08:40.040 --> 00:08:42.030
or make them ready for a photoshoot.

00:08:42.030 --> 00:08:44.420
He takes them exactly as they are

00:08:44.420 --> 00:08:46.220
and champions their specific flavors

00:08:46.220 --> 00:08:49.240
and often their unique flavor profiles.

00:08:49.240 --> 00:08:51.440
And while there are plenty
of classic chili dogs

00:08:51.440 --> 00:08:53.630
and homemade Twinkies
to be had on "Triple D,"

00:08:53.630 --> 00:08:57.000
it's, in a lot of ways,
remarkably inclusive.

00:08:57.000 --> 00:08:58.640
As far as the show is concerned,

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:02.150
any food made in America is
quintessentially American,

00:09:02.150 --> 00:09:03.890
especially when those cuisines originated

00:09:03.890 --> 00:09:04.810
from other countries.

00:09:04.810 --> 00:09:06.560
- [Guy] Greek favorites like hand-rolled,

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:08.260
beef-and-rice-filled grape leaves.

00:09:09.150 --> 00:09:10.730
- [Michael] In recent
years, you're more likely

00:09:10.730 --> 00:09:12.970
to see an interesting take on Vietnamese,

00:09:12.970 --> 00:09:15.070
Korean, or Mexican food on "Triple D"

00:09:15.070 --> 00:09:17.770
than you are a new iteration
of wings or burgers.

00:09:17.770 --> 00:09:19.980
- A Greek family making tacos in Texas!

00:09:19.980 --> 00:09:21.770
- [Michael] And bonus points
for foods that are mashups

00:09:21.770 --> 00:09:24.140
of different cultures, like pastrami tacos

00:09:24.140 --> 00:09:26.510
or duck confit enchiladas.

00:09:26.510 --> 00:09:29.967
That's a situation that
Fieri loves to describe as...

00:09:29.967 --> 00:09:30.800
- Ridiculous. Ridiculously good.

00:09:32.340 --> 00:09:34.610
- [Michael] Fieri's staked
his brand on an attitude

00:09:34.610 --> 00:09:38.090
of "I'm no better than
you; we just all love food.

00:09:38.090 --> 00:09:40.760
Food is the greatest common denominator."

00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:41.593
- Look at that meat.

00:09:41.593 --> 00:09:43.340
Tender, flaky, pull-aparty,

00:09:43.340 --> 00:09:45.430
super-hot-y, smolder-y, burny-mouth.

00:09:45.430 --> 00:09:47.380
- [Michael] This is a crucial
difference between him

00:09:47.380 --> 00:09:49.070
and most other food celebrities.

00:09:49.070 --> 00:09:52.230
On "Triple D," his expertise
is held in reserve.

00:09:52.230 --> 00:09:53.680
He doesn't make the rules,

00:09:53.680 --> 00:09:55.350
and he's not here to correct anyone.

00:09:55.350 --> 00:09:56.640
And while we might not think

00:09:56.640 --> 00:09:59.200
of Fieri as overtly philosophical,

00:09:59.200 --> 00:10:01.080
his sensibility towards food culture

00:10:01.080 --> 00:10:03.320
shares something with Scottish philosopher

00:10:03.320 --> 00:10:07.047
and legendary Edinburgh bar
patron David Hume, who said,

00:10:07.047 --> 00:10:09.240
"Indulge your passion for science,

00:10:09.240 --> 00:10:11.020
but let your science be human

00:10:11.020 --> 00:10:12.610
and such as may have a direct reference

00:10:12.610 --> 00:10:14.070
to action and society.

00:10:14.070 --> 00:10:16.610
Be a philosopher, but
amidst all your philosophy,

00:10:16.610 --> 00:10:18.510
be still a man."

00:10:18.510 --> 00:10:20.140
For Hume, this meant that deep

00:10:20.140 --> 00:10:22.070
and speculative thought was important,

00:10:22.070 --> 00:10:26.110
but so was experiencing life,
being social, and having fun.

00:10:26.110 --> 00:10:27.850
For him, philosophy was never meant

00:10:27.850 --> 00:10:30.650
to be at the expense of
relating to everyday life

00:10:30.650 --> 00:10:32.930
and, more bluntly, being a good hang.

00:10:32.930 --> 00:10:35.150
And part of the reason
Fieri's approach works

00:10:35.150 --> 00:10:38.370
is because he never gets
distracted by the more conceptual

00:10:38.370 --> 00:10:40.990
and intellectual aspects
of modern food culture.

00:10:40.990 --> 00:10:43.750
He's mostly there to chill and chow down.

00:10:43.750 --> 00:10:46.850
Fieri's version of Hume's
quote might be, "Be a chef,

00:10:46.850 --> 00:10:50.050
but not at the expense of
being a down-to-earth dude."

00:10:50.050 --> 00:10:51.770
Sure, while he's there, he talks

00:10:51.770 --> 00:10:55.090
about these supposedly lowbrow
foods in a highbrow way,

00:10:55.090 --> 00:10:57.330
and he makes it clear he
knows his way around a kitchen

00:10:57.330 --> 00:10:59.480
without acting like he owns the place.

00:10:59.480 --> 00:11:01.310
And it's precisely this approach

00:11:01.310 --> 00:11:04.230
that puts his culinary skills
somewhat in the background

00:11:04.230 --> 00:11:07.910
so the viewer can fully revel
in the sensory experience

00:11:07.910 --> 00:11:10.640
of eating a falafel waffle with him.

00:11:10.640 --> 00:11:12.070
Fieri's restaurant experience

00:11:12.070 --> 00:11:14.680
doesn't distract from the joy of eating.

00:11:14.680 --> 00:11:16.820
He doesn't roll into
these smaller businesses

00:11:16.820 --> 00:11:20.570
to throw his ego around,
unlike another chef we know.

00:11:20.570 --> 00:11:23.600
- Holy (beep). Rubber, tasteless.

00:11:23.600 --> 00:11:25.510
That's going straight to the trash.

00:11:25.510 --> 00:11:27.140
- [Michael] And he's even hella humble.

00:11:27.140 --> 00:11:28.010
- It's a great place.

00:11:28.010 --> 00:11:29.160
You got great people.

00:11:29.160 --> 00:11:31.270
This is the real diner
feel, so congratulations.

00:11:31.270 --> 00:11:33.930
- While he might get a lot
of flack for his own brand,

00:11:33.930 --> 00:11:35.660
most of what he's done on "Triple D"

00:11:35.660 --> 00:11:37.690
is give a massive amount of promotion

00:11:37.690 --> 00:11:40.040
to local spots he really believes in.

00:11:40.040 --> 00:11:41.780
As far as Fieri is concerned,

00:11:41.780 --> 00:11:43.280
everyone he meets is an expert

00:11:43.280 --> 00:11:45.800
when it comes to making
their signature dishes.

00:11:45.800 --> 00:11:48.140
And he has to show
deference to their recipes,

00:11:48.140 --> 00:11:50.670
because Fieri himself
has built a reputation

00:11:50.670 --> 00:11:53.400
on creating his own
unconventional menu items.

00:11:53.400 --> 00:11:55.970
He's not a pretentious culinary scold.

00:11:55.970 --> 00:11:57.440
He picked up most of his skills

00:11:57.440 --> 00:11:59.350
working in actual restaurants.

00:11:59.350 --> 00:12:01.620
He's positioned as a self-made man,

00:12:01.620 --> 00:12:03.530
and this bolsters his public appeal

00:12:03.530 --> 00:12:06.680
because Americans love a
bootstrapping narrative.

00:12:06.680 --> 00:12:09.130
All of these factors
combined make us wonder

00:12:09.130 --> 00:12:12.670
if Guy Fieri is the living,
breathing embodiment

00:12:12.670 --> 00:12:14.630
of the ideal American.

00:12:14.630 --> 00:12:17.980
Indeed, Fieri's devotion to
welcoming foods of all kinds

00:12:17.980 --> 00:12:19.970
into his show's vision of America suggests

00:12:19.970 --> 00:12:22.990
that he may be our true populist king.

00:12:22.990 --> 00:12:25.657
As cook and author Julia
Turshen wrote for "Lucky Peach,"

00:12:25.657 --> 00:12:28.170
"When it comes to visibility
and inclusiveness,

00:12:28.170 --> 00:12:30.620
'Diners, Drive-Ins and Dives' succeeds.

00:12:30.620 --> 00:12:32.780
Food allows the show to
highlight inclusivity

00:12:32.780 --> 00:12:34.826
without being about inclusivity."

00:12:34.826 --> 00:12:36.743
And yet, for segments of American culture

00:12:36.743 --> 00:12:39.140
that still would call Indian food exotic,

00:12:39.140 --> 00:12:41.290
Fieri appeals nonetheless.

00:12:41.290 --> 00:12:43.257
As Julia Moskin wrote
in the "New York Times,"

00:12:43.257 --> 00:12:46.510
"He has a Sarah Palin-like
ability to reach Americans

00:12:46.510 --> 00:12:48.850
who feel left behind by
the nation's cultural,

00:12:48.850 --> 00:12:50.700
or, in his case, culinary elite."

00:12:50.700 --> 00:12:51.800
Except, of course,

00:12:51.800 --> 00:12:55.480
Fieri has never claimed the
ability to see a foreign nation

00:12:55.480 --> 00:12:57.910
from outside of his
Northern California home.

00:12:57.910 --> 00:13:00.350
If most American celebrity
chefs teach people how

00:13:00.350 --> 00:13:03.810
to cook elevated food by,
say, adding French techniques,

00:13:03.810 --> 00:13:05.940
Fieri seeks to celebrate the American food

00:13:05.940 --> 00:13:07.960
that chefs are already cooking.

00:13:07.960 --> 00:13:11.430
Now, Fieri famously keeps mum
about his political opinions,

00:13:11.430 --> 00:13:13.440
though he'll do arguably political acts

00:13:13.440 --> 00:13:15.840
like fundraising nearly $25 million

00:13:15.840 --> 00:13:19.220
on behalf of unemployed restaurant
workers or cooking meals

00:13:19.220 --> 00:13:21.660
for volunteers fighting
California wildfires.

00:13:21.660 --> 00:13:23.837
As Matt Schimkowitz put
it in "The A.V. Club,"

00:13:23.837 --> 00:13:26.230
"Whether you read his
actions as philanthropic

00:13:26.230 --> 00:13:29.150
or socialist or the end of
food culture as we know it,

00:13:29.150 --> 00:13:30.560
Guy fits the bill.

00:13:30.560 --> 00:13:33.800
He shows up, puts on a show,
and leaves it to his audience

00:13:33.800 --> 00:13:35.910
to define who he is to them."

00:13:35.910 --> 00:13:39.180
And, indeed, Fieri's able to
cash in on his keen insight

00:13:39.180 --> 00:13:42.150
into the broader American
psyche by being everything

00:13:42.150 --> 00:13:45.100
and anything to everyone and anyone,

00:13:45.100 --> 00:13:47.660
regardless of background,
political affiliation,

00:13:47.660 --> 00:13:50.190
or access to flame-printed shirts.

00:13:50.190 --> 00:13:53.070
There's plenty of problems
with America, but ultimately,

00:13:53.070 --> 00:13:55.170
Fieri represents all the best parts

00:13:55.170 --> 00:13:56.870
of what the country claims to value:

00:13:56.870 --> 00:14:00.390
inclusivity, joy, and
on-fire, out-of-bounds,

00:14:00.390 --> 00:14:03.260
kicking, gangster, bomb flavor morsels.

00:14:03.260 --> 00:14:06.890
And that, in our opinion, is
actually, kinda, surprisingly,

00:14:06.890 --> 00:14:09.930
we can't believe we're
about to say this, deep.

00:14:09.930 --> 00:14:11.306
- You hear what I'm saying?

00:14:11.306 --> 00:14:12.270
- But what do you guys think?

00:14:12.270 --> 00:14:15.850
Is Guy Fieri the ultimate
embodiment of American ideals,

00:14:15.850 --> 00:14:17.540
or is he just a burger-lovin' dude

00:14:17.540 --> 00:14:20.130
who's single-handedly kept the
hair bleach industry afloat?

00:14:20.130 --> 00:14:21.800
Let us know in the comments.

00:14:21.800 --> 00:14:22.900
Hit the subscribe button

00:14:22.900 --> 00:14:25.080
like you're throwing too
much sauce on the meat,

00:14:25.080 --> 00:14:26.910
and don't forget to ring that bell.

00:14:26.910 --> 00:14:28.950
Thanks to our patrons
for all your support,

00:14:28.950 --> 00:14:32.680
and be sure to check out our
new exclusive perks on Patreon.

00:14:32.680 --> 00:14:35.458
And as always, thanks for watching. Later.

